  2  Verify the shift by printing token STRINGS, not ids
==============================================================================
  input token           ->  target token        (target must be the NEXT input)
    0  'First'              -> ' Citizen'          
    1  ' Citizen'           -> ':'                 
    2  ':'                  -> '\n'                
    3  '\n'                 -> 'Before'            
    4  'Before'             -> ' we'               
    5  ' we'                -> ' proceed'          
    6  ' proceed'           -> ' any'              
    7  ' any'               -> ' further'          
    8  ' further'           -> ','                 
    9  ','                  -> ' hear'             
   10  ' hear'              -> ' me'               
   11  ' me'                -> ' speak'            

  read as text:
    inputs : 'First Citizen:\nBefore we proceed any further, hear me speak.\n\nAll:\nSpeak, speak.'
    targets: ' Citizen:\nBefore we proceed any further, hear me speak.\n\nAll:\nSpeak, speak.\n'

  inputs[1:] == targets[:-1] ? True   <- this is the shift, asserted not eyeballed

  -- and the bug the lecture warns about twice: shifting the WRONG way --
     targets = tokens (no shift) hands the model its own input as the answer.
     inputs : 'First Citizen:\nBefore we proceed any further, hear me'
     targets: 'First Citizen:\nBefore we proceed any further, hear me'   <- identical. The loss will look wonderful.

==============================================================================
  3  Mask padding, and confirm the contributing-token count changes
==============================================================================
  sequence lengths in the batch : [64, 48, 28, 16]  (padded to 64)
  positions if padding counts   : 252
  positions after masking       : 152   (100 pad targets dropped, 40% of the batch)

  loss counting padding         : 9.2286
  loss ignoring padding         : 4.2502
  difference                    : -4.9784

  The count is the part the requirement asks for, and it changed: 252 -> 152.
  The loss moved too, but note which way. This model has never been trained on padded
  batches, so it assigns <pad> almost no probability and those positions are the
  EXPENSIVE ones -- masking them lowers the number. The famous trap is the opposite
  case, and it needs a model that has learned <pad>. Demonstrated below.

  -- the denominator bug, separately --
     Even with an ignore_index, dividing by B*T instead of the real count rescales the loss
     sum / n_real (152) = 4.2502   <- correct
     sum / B*T   (252) = 2.5636   <- wrong, and it moves with every batch

  -- and a distinction the lecture blurs: <eos> is NOT <pad> --
     <pad> is an artefact of batching and must be masked. <eos> is a real token the model
     has to learn to emit; mask it and the model never learns to stop.

  -- the trap, demonstrated --
     Above, the model has never seen <pad>, so masking it barely moves the number. The
     danger appears once the model has learned <pad> is trivially predictable. Train a
     copy on padded batches and measure both losses again:

  trained 300 steps on PADDED batches in 33s -> loss 2.7159

                                 counting pad   masking pad       gap
     before padded training            9.2286        4.2502   -4.9784
     after padded training             2.5965        4.0390    1.4425

     The counted loss fell 6.6321. The honest loss fell 0.2113.
     Only 3% of that apparent progress is real; the rest is the model
     learning to predict padding, which is worth nothing at inference.

==============================================================================
  4  Pack two documents into one sequence, mask the boundary
==============================================================================
  doc A: Shakespeare, 253 tokens -- 'itizen:\nSoft! who comes here?\n\n'...
  doc B: Python source, 312 tokens -- '"""A small GPT, deliberately boring.\n\n'...

  packed sequence               : 256 tokens, 7 document boundaries (2.7% of positions)
  loss WITHOUT boundary mask    : 6.8536
  loss WITH boundary mask       : 6.5536
  difference                    : -0.3000

  mean loss AT the 7 boundary positions : 17.4826
  mean loss at the 248 other positions   : 6.5536
  a boundary position is 2.67x as expensive as an ordinary one

  Explanation. A boundary position asks the model to predict the first token of a Python
  file from the last token of a Shakespeare speech. Nothing connects them, so the model
  cannot do better than its prior -- which is why those positions are the most expensive
  in the sequence, 2.67x an ordinary one.

  So masking them makes the reported loss FALL. That is not the loss being flattered:
  the positions removed were the ones teaching the model something false, namely that a
  Python file follows a Shakespeare speech. The unmasked number is the dishonest one --
  it is an average that includes a question with no answer, and it will drift with your
  packing density rather than with your model.

