  And Session 7 closes the escape: with a byte-codec input side there is no [V,D] input
  table to tie to, so 'just tie it' is not available to V5 at any price.

==============================================================================
  7  Peak memory: ordinary cross-entropy against a chunked one
==============================================================================
  Same objective, two implementations. Chunking computes `chunk` rows of logits at a
  time inside a checkpoint, so they are freed after the forward and recomputed during
  the backward. Arithmetic traded for memory; the loss must not move at all.

  this model's head: N=4,096 positions, D=384, V=50,257, bfloat16
    logits tensor = 393 MiB, 785 MiB once cross_entropy upcasts it to fp32

    implementation                peak MiB            loss   vs naive
    naive (all logits at once)      2650.5    10.910684586      1.00x
    chunked, chunk=1024              958.0    10.910684586      2.77x
    chunked, chunk=256               516.3    10.910684586      5.13x
    chunked, chunk=64                442.7    10.910687447      5.99x

    ratio naive : best chunked = 5.99x
    largest disagreement between any two losses: 2.86e-06
    floor (one row of logits, so essentially just the head + its gradient): 366.2 MiB
    best chunked sits 76.6 MiB above that floor

  ------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Again at the V5 vocabulary, which is where this stops being an optimisation and
  starts being the difference between a run that fits and one that does not.

