  wall clock: 170.8s on cuda

  head 1 (t+1): 10.8619 -> 3.7687   (-7.0932)
  head 2 (t+2): 10.8681 -> 4.4709   (-6.3972)
  sum         : 21.7300 -> 8.2396
  gap L2-L1   : +0.0062 -> +0.7021

  Both heads start at ln(V) = 10.8249. They have to: at step 0 neither knows anything,
  and 'the token after next' is exactly as unknown as 'the next token'.

  But look at the middle of the run. At steps [10, 20, 30, 40] the t+2 head
  is AHEAD of the t+1 head -- the harder question is being answered better. That should
  not happen, so it is worth finding out why rather than narrating past it.

  Hypothesis: it is not about the horizon at all, it is about TYING. Head 1 shares its
  matrix with the input embedding, so its gradient is fighting a second job; head 2 is a
  free matrix. Early in training, when both heads are mostly learning the marginal token
  frequencies, the unencumbered head fits that marginal faster.

  The hypothesis makes a prediction, so run the control: same everything, head 1 untied.

    step    tied: L2-L1    untied: L2-L1
       0        +0.0062          -0.0127
      10        -0.1358          -0.1209
      20        -0.1177          -0.0991
      30        -0.1201          -0.1061
      40        -0.0574          -0.0188
      50        +0.0168          +0.0246
      75        +0.1790          +0.1685
     100        +0.2480          +0.2600
     150        +0.3278          +0.3973
     200        +0.4593          +0.5238
     300        +0.5661          +0.6623
     450        +0.6439          +0.7302
     600        +0.7196          +0.8590
     750        +0.7060          +0.8240
     900        +0.7356          +0.8904
    1050        +0.7067          +0.8941
    1200        +0.7651          +0.9576
    1350        +0.7347          +0.9004
    1500        +0.7021          +0.9564

  tied   : gap negative at steps [10, 20, 30, 40]
  untied : gap negative at steps [0, 10, 20, 30, 40]

  REFUTED. The untied control crosses over at the same steps and by the same
  margin, so tying is not the cause. The hypothesis was wrong; the observation
  stands, and it is a property of the task pair rather than of the parameterisation.

  Second hypothesis: the crossover is where the model stops fitting the marginal
  and starts using context, which would put it at the corpus unigram entropy.
    unigram entropy of the training tokens : 6.3151 nats
    crossover happens between step 40 (L1=7.0928) and step 50 (L1=6.7028)
    is the unigram entropy inside that window? False

  ALSO REFUTED. The crossover happens at about 6.90 nats,
  roughly 0.58 nats ABOVE the marginal floor -- so it
  arrives before the model has even finished fitting unigram frequencies.

  Two hypotheses proposed, two refuted. What survives is the observation itself:
  reproducible under a fixed seed, identical in both arms, and crossing at the same
  step. The next discriminating test is a frozen random trunk -- if the effect is
  about how much context the trunk carries, freezing it should stop head 1 from ever
  overtaking. That is not run here, and it is not claimed either way.

  By the end the ordering is the expected one and the gap WIDENS as L1 falls. That gap is
  the point rather than a defect: head 2 must predict a token without ever seeing the one
  in between, so it forces the hidden state at t to carry information the next-token loss
  alone would never ask for. That is the extra supervision MTP is bought with -- and the
  cost is honest: another full [V,D] matrix, which at V5 scale is another 536.9M parameters.

